Invertibility of the weight matrix: put $X_{\mu
u}:=\dim_F(L^{\mu}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})$ for $\mu,
u\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$. Feeding a composition series of $W^{\lambda}$ into step 2.1 gives $$K_{\lambda
u}=\dim_F(W^{\lambda}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})=\sum_{\mu}d_{\lambda\mu}X_{\mu
u},$$ that is, $K=dX$ with $d=(d_{\lambda\mu})$ and $K=(K_{\lambda
u})$. Both $d$ and $K$ are unitriangular over $\mathbb Z$ by [F1] and [F2] respectively, hence invertible over $\mathbb Z$, and therefore $X=d^{-1}K$ is invertible over $\mathbb Z$.

Conclusion: write $c_{\mu}:=[M:L^{\mu}]$ and $b_{\mu}:=c_{\mu}-\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}d_{\lambda\mu}\in\mathbb Z$. By step 2.1 applied to $M$ and to the identity $\dim_F(W^{\lambda}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})=K_{\lambda
u}$, for every $
u$ $$0=\dim_F(M\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})-\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}\dim_F(W^{\lambda}\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})=\sum_{\mu}c_{\mu}X_{\mu
u}-\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}\sum_{\mu}d_{\lambda\mu}X_{\mu
u}=\sum_{\mu}b_{\mu}X_{\mu
u}.$$ Since $X$ is invertible over $\mathbb Z$ by step 3.1, $b_{\mu}=0$ for all $\mu$. Hence $c_{\mu}=\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}d_{\lambda\mu}$, and step 2.1 together with the defining relations [F3] gives $$[M]=\sum_{\mu}c_{\mu}[L^{\mu}]=\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}\sum_{\mu}d_{\lambda\mu}[L^{\mu}]=\sum_{\lambda}a_{\lambda}[W^{\lambda}].$$

Consequence: if two finite-dimensional right $S$-modules $M,N$ satisfy $\dim_F(M\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})=\dim_F(N\varphi_{T^{
u}T^{
u}})$ for all $
u\in\Lambda^{+}(d,n)$, then applying the argument of step 4.1 to the difference of their multiplicity vectors shows $\sum_{\mu}([M:L^{\mu}]-[N:L^{\mu}])X_{\mu
u}=0$ for all $
u$, so the invertibility of $X$ gives $[M:L^{\mu}]=[N:L^{\mu}]$ for all $\mu$ and hence $[M]=[N]$ in $G_0(S	ext{-mod})$.
